from core import subjects,L
# Additional explanations focus on derivations and reasoning, rather than adding repeated summaries.
def deepen(id,title,body,code=None):
 l=next(l for s in subjects for l in s['lessons'] if l['id']==id)
 l['sections'].append({'title':title,'body':body,**({'code':code} if code else {})})

deepen('0-propositions','Necessary versus sufficient: translating words',
'“P only if Q” means that whenever P holds, Q must hold: P→Q. “P if Q” means Q is enough to establish P: Q→P. For example, “a number is divisible by 4 only if it is even” states divisibility by 4 implies evenness; the reverse is refuted by 6. A necessary condition can hold even when the target property does not. To test a proposed equivalence, translate each direction separately and look for a counterexample. In proofs, modus ponens uses P and P→Q to infer Q, while modus tollens uses ¬Q and P→Q to infer ¬P. Inferring P merely from Q is the invalid pattern called affirming the consequent.')
deepen('0-quantifiers','Choosing witnesses and refuting nested statements',
'For ∀x∃y R(x,y), an explicit witness formula y=g(x) can prove the claim. To refute it, find one x for which every possible y fails. For ∃y∀x R(x,y), the witness must be chosen independently of x; refuting it requires showing that each candidate y fails for some x. Over integers, ∀x∃y(y>x) is true with y=x+1, but ∃y∀x(y>x) is false because for any proposed y, choosing x=y violates strict inequality. These are different orders of dependency, not different spellings of the same statement. When translating “only students passed,” write Passed(x)→Student(x); it does not assert that all students passed.')
deepen('0-sets-functions','Relation matrices and composition',
'For a finite set {a₁,…,aₙ}, represent a relation by a Boolean matrix M where Mᵢⱼ=1 iff aᵢRaⱼ. Reflexivity requires diagonal 1s; symmetry requires M=Mᵀ; antisymmetry disallows simultaneous off-diagonal 1s in symmetric positions. Transitivity requires that every two-edge path implies its direct relation pair. Relation composition uses Boolean matrix multiplication, where products use AND and sums use OR. The transitive closure adds all pairs reachable by paths, and need not add loops unless a path or reflexive closure requires them. For function composition g∘f, apply f first and then g; the right-hand function acts first. If both functions are injective, their composition is injective, but properties of a composition do not freely imply every property of each component.')
deepen('0-orders-lattices','Finding bounds without confusing numeric order',
'To find an upper bound of a,b in an arbitrary poset, list elements x for which a≤x and b≤x according to the defined relation. Among these candidates, a least upper bound must be below every other upper bound. A merely minimal upper bound might fail that requirement; two incomparable minimal upper bounds mean there is no join. For a Hasse diagram, follow upward paths from both elements and intersect their reachable sets. Repeat downward for lower bounds. In a finite poset, having a top and bottom alone does not make it a lattice: the pairwise meet and join requirement still needs checking. In subset inclusion, these constructions reduce to union and intersection because those sets satisfy the universal bound definitions.')
deepen('0-algebraic-structures','Cancellation and element order',
'Group inverses justify cancellation: from a*b=a*c, multiply on the left by a⁻¹ and use associativity to obtain b=c. A general monoid need not permit this. The order of a group element is the least positive k for which aᵏ=e, if such a k exists; this means repeated application of the group operation, so under addition use repeated sums. In addition modulo 6, element 2 has order 3 because 2+2+2≡0, while neither one nor two repetitions is zero. For a finite group, the order of an element divides group size by Lagrange’s theorem. The theorem gives a necessary divisibility condition, not a guarantee that every divisor is realized by an element in every group.')
deepen('0-graphs-matching','Chromatic bounds and matching certificates',
'A clique of size k forces at least k vertex colours because every pair in it is adjacent. Conversely a greedy colouring uses at most Δ+1 colours, where Δ is maximum degree: when colouring a vertex, at most Δ neighbour colours can be forbidden. These are bounds, not generally equal values. For a path with at least one edge, alternating two colours proves χ=2. A matching can be improved along an augmenting path whose edges alternate unmatched and matched, beginning and ending at unmatched vertices. Flipping membership along that path increases matching size by one. An absent augmenting path certifies maximum matching under the standard matching theorem; merely being unable to add a single isolated edge certifies only maximality.')
deepen('0-counting','Inclusion–exclusion with three sets',
'For three events or finite sets, |A∪B∪C|=|A|+|B|+|C|−|A∩B|−|A∩C|−|B∩C|+|A∩B∩C|. An element in all three was initially counted three times, then subtracted three times, so it needs adding once. This accounting explains the signs rather than just memorizing them. To count positive solutions to x+y+z=7, set u=x−1,v=y−1,w=z−1. Then u+v+w=4 with nonnegative variables, so there are C(6,2)=15 solutions. Pigeonhole statements also need tight boxes: among 13 people, two share a birth month, but this does not mean exactly two do or identify which month.')
deepen('0-recurrences','Characteristic-root derivation',
'For aₙ=3aₙ₋₁−2aₙ₋₂, try a solution λⁿ. Substitution and division by λⁿ⁻² gives λ²−3λ+2=0, whose roots are 1 and 2. Therefore aₙ=A+B2ⁿ for distinct roots. With a₀=0,a₁=1, solve A+B=0 and A+2B=1 to obtain B=1,A=−1, hence aₙ=2ⁿ−1. Initial values select the constants; the recurrence alone specifies many possible sequences. With a repeated root λ, the independent solutions include λⁿ and nλⁿ. A nonhomogeneous recurrence needs a particular solution in addition to homogeneous solutions; a trial form overlapping the homogeneous solution may require multiplication by n.')
deepen('0-matrices-rank','Elimination as a reproducible procedure',
'Choose a nonzero pivot in the current column, swap a row into the pivot position if needed, and eliminate entries below it. Move to the next pivot row and later column, skipping columns that cannot supply a pivot. Row operations must apply to the augmented right-hand side as well as coefficients. For x+y=3 and x−y=1, subtract the second equation from the first to obtain 2y=2, so y=1 and x=2. To find an inverse, augment A with I and row-reduce [A|I] to [I|A⁻¹]. Failure to reach a pivot in each column of square A proves singularity. Determinant changes must be tracked if using elimination to compute det(A).')
deepen('0-eigen-lu','Repeated eigenvalues and independent directions',
'The matrix [[2,1],[0,2]] has characteristic polynomial (2−λ)², but solving (A−2I)v=0 forces v₂=0. Its eigenspace has dimension one, so two independent eigenvectors do not exist and the matrix is not diagonalizable. By contrast 2I has the same repeated eigenvalue but every nonzero vector is an eigenvector, giving a two-dimensional eigenspace. Algebraic multiplicity counts characteristic roots; geometric multiplicity is nullity of A−λI. The geometric multiplicity cannot exceed algebraic multiplicity. For repeated linear solves using LU, factorization costs roughly cubic work for dense matrices while each triangular solve costs quadratic work, explaining why reusing factors is beneficial.')
deepen('0-limits-continuity','Piecewise continuity and matching slopes',
'Consider f(x)=ax+b for x<1 and f(x)=x² for x≥1. Right-side value and limit at 1 are both 1. Continuity requires a+b=1. The left derivative is a while the right derivative is 2, so differentiability additionally requires a=2, hence b=−1. Matching values alone leaves many continuous choices, but only the matching slope gives differentiability here. For limits involving infinity, divide polynomial numerator and denominator by their highest relevant power of x to compare growth. A limit can exist even when the function is undefined at the point. Neither observing a plot nor substituting a few nearby values proves the exact limiting behaviour.')
deepen('0-extrema-mvt','Classifying a stationary point by signs',
'For f(x)=x³−3x, derivative is 3x²−3, vanishing at x=−1 and x=1. On x<−1 the derivative is positive, on −1<x<1 it is negative, and on x>1 it is positive. Thus −1 is a local maximum and 1 a local minimum. The second derivative 6x gives the same classification, but the sign test also handles cases where f″ is zero. To find absolute extrema over a given closed interval, compare only critical points inside that interval plus its endpoints. Rolle’s theorem is MVT with equal endpoint values: the average slope becomes zero. A nondifferentiable corner can invalidate the hypothesis even if endpoints match.')
deepen('0-integration','Integration by parts with actual evaluation',
'To evaluate ∫₀¹xeˣdx, choose u=x and dv=eˣdx. Then du=dx and v=eˣ. Integration by parts gives [xeˣ]₀¹−∫₀¹eˣdx=e−(e−1)=1. The choice simplifies the polynomial factor under differentiation. For area between a curve and the x-axis, split intervals at sign changes and integrate absolute contributions; the integral of x on [−1,1] is zero but geometric area is 1. An improper integral such as ∫₁∞1/x²dx is a limit: ∫₁ᴹ1/x²dx=1−1/M tends to 1. ∫₁∞1/x dx instead grows as ln M and diverges; formally writing an antiderivative is not proof of convergence.')
deepen('0-conditional-bayes','A table prevents reversed conditioning',
'Use rows for cause classes and columns for evidence outcomes. For prevalence 0.2, sensitivity 0.8 and false-positive rate 0.1, among 1000 cases there are 200 positives-by-condition, of which 160 test positive; among the 800 without the condition, 80 test positive. Hence P(test positive)=240/1000 and P(condition|test positive)=160/240=2/3. The denominator is everyone with the evidence, not everyone with the condition. The formula agrees: 0.8×0.2/[0.8×0.2+0.1×0.8]. Independence can also be tested by checking whether conditioning leaves the probability unchanged. Sampling without replacement often destroys that equality because the first draw changes the remaining population.')
deepen('0-random-statistics','Deriving variance and using linearity',
'Variance is E[(X−μ)²], where μ=E[X]. Expand the square: E[X²−2μX+μ²]=E[X²]−2μE[X]+μ²=E[X²]−μ². This explains why the second moment, not just the mean, is needed. For a count X=I₁+…+Iₙ of indicator variables, E[X]=ΣE[Iᵢ]=ΣP(Iᵢ=1), even when the events are dependent. Variance addition requires checking covariance: Var(X+Y)=Var(X)+Var(Y)+2Cov(X,Y). Independent variables have covariance zero, but zero covariance alone does not establish independence in general. Scaling all observations by 3 scales deviations by 3 and squared deviations by 9, while adding a constant leaves deviations unchanged.')
deepen('0-binomial-poisson','Deriving binomial moments with indicators',
'Write the success count X as the sum of n independent Bernoulli indicators Iᵢ. Each has mean p and variance p(1−p), so expectation is np and independent variances sum to np(1−p). The mass formula contains C(n,k) because there are that many ways to place k successes among n positions. For Poisson with rate 3/hour, a 20-minute interval has λ=1, not λ=3: converting the window is part of specifying the distribution. Then P(exactly two)=e⁻¹/2. A binomial n=1000,p=0.001 has mean 1; its zero-count probability (0.999)^1000 is close to e⁻¹, illustrating the rare-event approximation without treating it as an exact equality.')
deepen('0-continuous-distributions','Normalize a density before calculating probabilities',
'If f(x)=cx on [0,2] and zero outside, normalization requires ∫₀²cx dx=2c=1, so c=1/2. Then P(1<X<2)=∫₁²x/2 dx=3/4, and E[X]=∫₀²x²/2 dx=4/3. This distribution is not uniform because density grows with x. For exponential waiting time, conditional survival divides two survival probabilities and cancels the elapsed factor, explaining memorylessness directly. For normal variables, convert an interval using standard deviation before looking up Φ. If Y=aX+b for a negative a, inequality direction reverses when standardizing an event; keeping the event itself visible avoids sign errors.')

deepen('3-c-memory','A safe pointer trace',
'Use a table of variable, type, value and referenced object. In the example, a is an array object, p points to its second element, and *p refers to a[1]. After *p=9, the array changes without changing p. Moving p by one advances to a[2]; it does not increase the integer stored at a[1]. A pointer may be formed one past the array, but that location is not an element to dereference. The code deliberately separates updates into sequenced statements to avoid unsequenced modification traps.',
'''int a[] = {2, 4, 6};
int *p = &a[1];
*p = 9;        /* a becomes {2,9,6} */
p = p + 1;     /* p points to a[2] */
/* *p is now 6 */''')
deepen('3-recursion','A recursion template you can trace',
'The base case returns before making another call. For n>0, n−1 is a smaller nonnegative input, so the decreasing argument guarantees reaching zero. The multiplication waits for the child call to return. This mathematical template assumes the answer fits in the return type: a real C implementation can overflow for sufficiently large n, and the allowed range must be part of its contract.',
'''unsigned long long factorial(unsigned n) {
    if (n == 0) return 1;
    return n * factorial(n - 1);
}''')
deepen('3-linear-structures','Linked queue operation sequence',
'To enqueue a node, initialize its next pointer to NULL, attach it after the current tail if one exists, or make it the head for an empty queue; then update tail. To dequeue, read the head node, advance head to its next, and if the queue becomes empty set tail to NULL as well. Save the returned value before freeing the removed node. These pointer operations are O(1) because no predecessor search is required. A nonempty invariant is that tail points to the final node and tail.next is NULL. The empty invariant has both pointers NULL. Missing the final tail reset leaves a dangling pointer after removing the last node.')
deepen('3-trees-bst','Why traversal order changes output',
'The recursive structure is the same for all three depth-first traversals; only the placement of visit(root) changes. Put it before the two recursive calls for preorder, between them for inorder, or afterward for postorder. On a BST, the between placement visits every smaller left key, then the root, then every larger right key, proving sorted output by induction. All three traversals visit each node once and take Θ(n) time; auxiliary recursive space is Θ(h), which is Θ(n) for a chain and Θ(log n) for a balanced tree.',
'''inorder(node):
    if node is null: return
    inorder(node.left)
    visit(node.key)
    inorder(node.right)''')
deepen('3-heaps-graphs','Why build-heap is linear',
'Only about n/2 nodes have height zero, at most n/4 have height one, at most n/8 have height two, and so on. A sift from height h costs O(h), so aggregate work is bounded by n times Σh/2^(h+1), a convergent constant sum. Thus bottom-up build is O(n), unlike repeatedly inserting n elements, whose simple worst-case bound is O(n log n). The algorithm starts at the last internal node, floor(n/2)−1 in zero-based indexing, and visits parents backward. Each node’s child subtrees are already heaps before it is sifted, which is the key invariant.')
deepen('4-analysis','A recursion-tree comparison',
'T(n)=T(n/2)+1 does constant work at each of log₂n levels, giving Θ(log n). T(n)=2T(n/2)+1 has increasingly many nodes and Θ(n) total calls. T(n)=2T(n/2)+n has total n work at each of logarithmically many levels, giving Θ(n log n). All three have logarithmic depth, demonstrating why depth is not a time bound. For an iterative doubling loop i=1;i<n;i*=2, iterations also grow logarithmically; a triangular incrementing loop instead requires summing linearly increasing counts. Specify input size in units that match the algorithm: numeric value n and bit length log n are not the same complexity parameter.')
deepen('4-search-sort','Binary search with terminating bounds',
'This version keeps an inclusive range [lo,hi]. On a failed comparison it removes the midpoint as well as the impossible half, ensuring progress even when one or two candidates remain. The midpoint formula avoids unnecessarily adding two large indices in fixed-width arithmetic. The returned −1 distinguishes absence from valid zero-based index 0. Sorting the input is a prerequisite; this routine does not verify that condition.',
'''binary_search(a, key):
    lo = 0; hi = length(a) - 1
    while lo <= hi:
        mid = lo + floor((hi-lo)/2)
        if a[mid] == key: return mid
        if a[mid] < key: lo = mid + 1
        else: hi = mid - 1
    return -1''')
deepen('4-hashing','A probing trace with wraparound',
'With capacity 7 and h(k)=k mod 7, insert 6,13,20,27 by linear probing. All initially choose slot 6. The first occupies 6; the second wraps to 0; the third checks 6,0 and occupies 1; the fourth occupies 2. This creates a cluster spanning the boundary. Searching for absent key 34 probes those occupied slots before reaching empty slot 3. The cost depends on occupied runs, not simply whether its initial hash is in range. A tombstone should not stop unsuccessful search, but insertion may remember its position for reuse while continuing sufficiently far to rule out an existing duplicate key.')
deepen('4-dynamic','0/1 knapsack in one dimension',
'Process capacities downward so dp[w−weight] still refers to the state before the current item was introduced. An upward loop could read a value updated earlier during the same item and count that item again. For a single item weight 2,value 3 and capacity 4, downward iteration gives dp[4]=3 and dp[2]=3; upward iteration can incorrectly produce dp[4]=6 for a 0/1 problem.',
'''dp = array[0..W] filled with 0
for each item (weight, value):
    for w from W down to weight:
        dp[w] = max(dp[w], value + dp[w-weight])''')
